Simple Index is a basic tool to help you with creating commands easily in Roblox.
Use "-" to open the Command bar.

Here is the module:
local SimpleIndex = loadstring(game:HttpGet("https://raw.githubusercontent.com/MasterNoob6291/SimpleIndex/main/SimpleIndex.lua"))()

---------------------- COMMMANDS ----------------------
Creating commands are pretty simple and take little effort.
Here is an example:

SimpleIndex.command("example", function(args)
  -- Function inside of here
end, "Example command")

Running this after the Module will create an Example command.
So, typing: "-example" will exacute the command.

-- Arguments --
These commands can take in Arguments. The args parameter is a table.
For example, this will output the first argument you type after the command:

SimpleIndex.command("example", function(args)
  print(args[1])
end, "Example command")

So, lets say you type "-example Hello"
This will print "Hello" in the console.

-- Description --

The description is the first part after the end.
If you type "-cmds" in the Command bar you will get a list of commands along with their descriptions.
Descriptions can be useful to tell your player how the command works.
For example the description of the example command is "Example command"
If you type "-cmds" and check console it will have the example command followed by the description.

---------------------- AutoCompletes ----------------------

Auto completes are a list of commands you can type to help you auto complete a command.
Lets say you type part of the example command "-exa" The auto complete will show "-example" following by anything that starts with "-exa"
You can press space and it will complete the first auto complete that it thinks you were trying to type.
The module does this for you, but what if you wanted to create auto completes for arguments.
For example "-kick" and then you want to auto complete usernames in the game for players to kick.
Well you can do that using the Auto Complete function:

local Players = game.Players()
SimpleIndex.AutocompleteTypes.player = SimpleIndex.AutocompleteTypes.simple(function(input, used)
	local matches = {}

	for _, plr in ipairs(Players:GetPlayers()) do
		local name = plr.Name
		if name:lower():sub(1, #input) == input:lower() and not used[name:lower()] then
			table.insert(matches, name)
		end
	end

	for _, special in ipairs({ "me", "others", "random", "all" }) do
		if special:sub(1, #input) == input:lower() and not used[special] then
			table.insert(matches, special)
		end
	end

	return matches
end)

This does exactly that.
This creates a list of matches, or in this case players to auto complete.
The "input" argument is an argument you typed in after the command. (The "used" argument is not important for this function)
So how do you use it.
Lets say you want to kick the username of a player.

SimpleIndex.command("kick", function(args)
	if args == nil then return end
	for i, v in pairs(SimpleIndex.getTargetPlayers(args[1])) do
		print("Kicking:", v)
	end
end, "Kicks player.", { [1] = "player" })

The "{ [1] = "player" }" after the description is how to use it.
You call the AutoComplete shown above and the "[1]" is the argument place you picked. So "[1]" is the first argument after the command.
To create another Auto Complete you can change the AutocompleteTypes.player to AutocompleteTypes. -- What ever you want afterwards.

-- Extras --
In the kick command example we had a "getTargetPlayers" function.
This is here to make it simple to do things like kick. It takes in a string and gets a table of players from it.
That means it can take in "all" "others" "random" or "me" along with typing PART of someones username. 
You do not have to type someones full username for this function to work. 

---------------------- NOTIFICATIONS ----------------------
Notifications are boxes that to notify the player something.
For example:

SimpleIndex.Notify("Example Heading", "Example Message", 3)

The first string is your Heading. So "Error" or "Warning"
The second is the message. So "Something went wrong" for example.
The last argument is the time the notification will stay visible.

